{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Software Design Patterns - Behavioral Patterns","content":"# Software Design Patterns - Behavioral Patterns\n\nBehavioral patterns deal with efficient communication between objects and the delegation of responsibilities.\n\nThe behavioral patterns covered here are: Chain of Responsibility, Command, Iterator, Mediator, Memento, Observer, State, Strategy, Template Method, and Visitor.\n\n## Chain of Responsibility (CoR)[ #](#chain-of-responsibility-cor)\n\nThe Chain of Responsibility pattern lets you pass a request along a chain of handlers. When a handler at any node receives the request, it can either process it or pass it on to the next handler in the chain.\n\nFor example:\n\n![CoR](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/3bd87b0f387e.png)\n\nThis is just a two-level chain.\n\nSample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Handler {\npublic:\n    void handle() = 0;\n};\n\nclass BasicHandler : public Handler {\npublic:\n    void handle() {\n        // TODO: perform the first-level handling\n        if (okay && next != nullptr) {\n            next(); // pass to the next handler in the chain\n        }\n    }\n    void setNext(Handler& hdr) {\n        next = hdr\n    }\nprivate:\n    Handler next;\n};\n\nclass SecondHandler : public Handler {\npublic:\n    void handle() {\n        // TODO: perform the required handling\n    }\n    // the last handler does not need a next\n    // void setNext(Handler& hdr) {\n    //     next = hdr\n    // }\nprivate:\n    // Handler next;\n};\n```\n\n## Command[ #](#command)\n\nThe Command pattern turns a request into a standalone object that contains all the information about that request. These objects are kept in a queue, and queued elements can be undone.\n\nTake the design of an editor application as an example:\n\n![Command](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/864e9e18f296.png)\n\nSample code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Command {\npublic:\n    void execute() = 0;\n};\n\n// Open file\nclass OpenCommand : public Command {\npublic:\n    void execute() {\n        // Perform the file-open operation\n    }\n};\n\n// Save file\nclass SaveCommand : public Command {\npublic:\n    void execute() {\n        // Perform the file-save operation\n    }\n};\n\n// Print file\nclass PrintCommand : public Command {\npublic:\n    void execute() {\n        // Perform the file-print operation\n    }\n};\n```\n\nWe also need a command queue mechanism:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\n// Command queue\nclass CommandQueue {\npublic:\n    void push(Command&) {\n        // Enqueue a command\n    }\n    Command pop() {\n        // Dequeue a command\n    }\n};\n```\n\nThe command queue stores commands, and enqueuing and dequeuing follow some scheduling algorithm:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nCommandQueue cq(10);\n\n// Any scheduling strategy works; a timer is used here\nwhile (true) {\n    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));\n    auto cmd = cq.pop();\n    cmd.execute();\n}\n```\n\nTo undo, simply pop the element off the queue without executing it:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\ncq.pop();\n```\n\nCreating an \"Open\" menu action only takes the following:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nauto openCmd = new OpenCommand();\nauto menuOpen = new Menu(\"Open\");\nmenuOpen.action = [openCmd](){\n    cq.push(openCmd); // push the open command onto the queue\n};\n```\n\n## Iterator[ #](#iterator)\n\nThe goal of the Iterator pattern is to let outside code traverse every element of a collection without exposing the collection's underlying representation.\n\nThe structure looks like this:\n\n![Iterator](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/bab81b954a9e.png)\n\nFirst, define a collection:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\n// Iterator\nclass Iterator {\npublic:\n    Iterator next() = 0;\n};\n\nclass Item : public Iterator {\npublic:\n    Item next() {\n        // Next element\n    }\n};\n\n// Iterable collection\nclass IteratorCollection {\npublic:\n    Iterator create(Iterator) = 0;\n    Iterator begin() = 0;\n    Iterator end() = 0;\n};\n\nclass Collection : public IteratorCollection {\npublic:\n    Iterator create(Iterator) {\n        // Create an iterator\n    }\n    Iterator begin() {\n        // Return an iterator to the first element\n    }\n    Iterator end() {\n        // Return an iterator to the last element\n    }\n};\n```\n\nThe iterator collection can be built like this:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nCollection collection;\nItem empty;\nfirst = collection.create(empty);\nsecond = collection.create(first);\nthird = collection.create(second);\n```\n\nThere are many ways to build an iterator collection: one element at a time through a function as above, or all at once through a constructor.\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nCollection collection([1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8]);\n```\n\nUse the iterator collection to access the iterator elements:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nfor (auto it = collection.begin(); it != collection.end(); it = it.next()) {\n    // Access the element via iterator it\n}\n```\n\n## Mediator[ #](#mediator)\n\nThe Mediator pattern reduces chaotic, tangled dependencies between objects. It restricts direct interaction between objects and has them interact through a mediator object instead.\n\nConsider a form UI where the user fills in name, date of birth, gender, and so on. Updating one field also triggers changes in other UI elements; for example, entering a date of birth updates the age in the form. If every UI control talked to the others directly to update them, the result would eventually be a mess. A mediator prevents that mess.\n\n![Mediator](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/1decbdf92c84.png)\n\nAs shown above, changing the date of birth updates the age, and routing this through the mediator avoids the chaos caused by direct calls.\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Mediator {\npublic:\n    void notify(int type, std::string param) = 0;\n    bool registerObject() = 0;\n};\n\nclass UserInformation {\npublic:\n    void notify(int type, std::string param) {\n        // Notify different objects to update the UI based on type\n    }\n    bool registerObject() {\n        // Register a UI object\n    }\nprivate:\n    std::vector<UIObject> objects;\n};\n```\n\nIn use, the in-memory environment keeps a single user-information object, defined like this:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nUserInformation info;\ninfo.registerObject(userNameLabel);\ninfo.registerObject(birthLabel);\ninfo.registerObject(ageLabel);\n// ...\n```\n\nWhen the user changes the value of a UI control:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\ninfo.notify(123, \"1975-02-12\"); // assume 123 is the \"birth date updated\" event; the argument is the new date\n```\n\n## Memento[ #](#memento)\n\nThe Memento pattern lets you save and restore an object's previous state without exposing its implementation details.\n\nEditor applications generally support undo and redo, which can be implemented like this:\n\n![Memento](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/adea908520cc.png)\n\nIn the diagram, opening a document creates an initial originator. The next snapshot is based on that originator, and so on. Snapshots are stored and managed in a snapshot list. The code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Memento {\npublic:\n    void restore() = 0;\n};\n\nclass Originator {\npublic:\n    Memento* save() = 0;\n};\n\nclass Snapshot : public Memento {\npublic:\n    Snapshot(Memento* m, int s) {\n        originator = m;\n        state = s;\n        // Save the operation state\n    }\n    void restore() {\n        // Restore the operation state\n    }\n};\n\nclass Initial : public Originator {\npublic:\n    void setState(int state) {\n        this->state = state;\n    }\n    Memento* save() {\n        return Snapshot(this, state);\n    }\nprivate:\n    int state;\n};\n```\n\nDefine a caretaker that manages snapshots, creating and restoring them:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nstd::vector<Snapshot*> snapshots;\n// Initialize the originator\nInitial init;\ninit.setState(0);\n// Save a snapshot\nSnapshot* s1 = new Snapshot(init, 1);\nsnapshots.push_back(s1);\n// Restore the snapshot\nauto s = snapshots.pop_back();\ns->restore();\n```\n\n## Observer[ #](#observer)\n\nThe Observer pattern is a publish-subscribe mechanism: when an object's state changes, it notifies multiple other \"observing\" objects.\n\nTake a code editor as an example. A code editor is not just a text editor; it also supports syntax highlighting, auto-indentation, automatic bracket matching, and other features driven by the code text.\n\n![Observer](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/9d980a98bd2d.png)\n\nThe implementation:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass EventListener {\npublic:\n    void update() = 0;\n};\n\nclass Highlight : public EventListener {\npublic:\n    void update() {\n        // Update syntax highlighting\n    }\n};\n\nclass AutoIndent : public EventListener {\npublic:\n    void update() {\n        // Update auto-indentation\n    }\n};\n\nclass AutoBracket : public EventListener {\npublic:\n    void update() {\n        // Update bracket matching\n    }\n};\n\nclass EventManager {\npublic:\n    void subscribe(int type, EventListener* listener) {\n        auto it = listeners[type];\n        if (it == nullptr) {\n            it = initListenerList(); // initialize the listener list\n            listeners.insert(std::make_pair(type, it));\n        }\n        it->push_back(listener); // add the listener\n    }\n    void unsubscribe(int type, EventListener* listener) {\n        auto it = listeners[type];\n        if (it == nullptr) return;\n        if (find_if(it, listener)) { // look up the listener\n            it = remove_if(it, listener); // remove it\n            listeners[type] = it;\n        }\n    }\n    void notify(int type, DataFrame* data) {\n        // Iterate over all listeners of this event and notify them\n        auto it = listeners[type];\n        if (it == nullptr) return;\n        for (l := it->begin(); l != it->end(); l = l->next()) {\n            l->update(); // notify the event\n        }\n    }\nprivate:\n    std::map<int, ListenerList*> listeners;\n};\n\nclass Editor {\npublic:\n    Editor() {}\nprivate:\n    EventManager eventMgr;\n};\n```\n\n## State[ #](#state)\n\nThe State pattern means that when an object's state changes, its behavior changes too. The core idea comes from finite state machines.\n\nA thermostat-controlled air conditioner is one of the simplest state machines, as shown below:\n\n![State Machine](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/7a92462341cc.png)\n\nThe class definition for the state model:\n\n![State](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/daa6b086d079.png)\n\nThe code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass AirConditionerState {\npublic:\n    AirConditionerState() {\n        // Initialize the state transition table\n        transits.insert(compound(STATE_START, SYM_RDY), STATE_ON);\n        transits.insert(compound(STATE_ON, SYM_BELOW), STATE_WAIT);\n        // ...\n    }\n    void transit(int cur) { // transition function\n        auto sym = cur > 26 ? SYM_ABOVE : SYM_BELOW;\n        auto key = compound(state, sym);\n        auto val = transits.find(key);\n        state = val;\n    }\nprivate:\n    int state;\n    std::map<int, int> transits;\n\n    inline int compound(int state, int symbol) {\n        return state | (symbol << 16);\n    }\n};\n```\n\nOn each call, just pass the current temperature to `transit()`. The function switches states in the state table based on the current state and the input symbol.\n\n> How state machines work\n>\n> > A state machine consists of a symbol table, a state table, a transition function, and start and end states. In one sentence: a state machine uses symbols from the symbol table to move between states in the state table via the transition function. State machines are divided into finite and infinite state machines; an infinite state machine's state table differs in size from a finite one's and may contain infinitely many states. Finite state machines are used in compilers, both for lexical analysis and for building formal syntax analysis.\n\n## Strategy[ #](#strategy)\n\nThe Strategy pattern lets an algorithm be customized. For example, suppose some software supports machine learning. To use it, we choose a model and train it, then use it for real predictions later. The model can be abstracted as a strategy, and choosing among models is choosing among strategies.\n\n![Strategy](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/efdffe0eb9a6.png)\n\nThe implementation:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass Strategy {\npublic:\n    void train() = 0;\n    void predict() = 0;\n};\n\nclass LinearRegression : public Strategy {\npublic:\n    void train() {\n        // Linear regression training\n    }\n    void predict() {\n        // Linear regression prediction\n    }\n};\n\nclass DecisionTree : public Strategy {\npublic:\n    void train() {\n        // Decision tree training\n    }\n    void predict() {\n        // Decision tree prediction\n    }\n};\n\nclass Forest : public Strategy {\npublic:\n    void train() {\n        // Random forest training\n    }\n    void predict() {\n        // Random forest prediction\n    }\n};\n\nclass NeuralNetwork : public Strategy {\npublic:\n    void train() {\n        // Neural network training\n    }\n    void predict() {\n        // Neural network prediction\n    }\n};\n\nclass AIDecision {\npublic:\n    void setStrategy(Strategy* s) {\n        strategy = s;\n        scheduleTrain();\n    }\n    void decisionPredict() {\n        strategy->predict();\n    }\nprivate:\n    Strategy* strategy;\n};\n```\n\nA caller uses it like this:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nAIDecision ai;\n\n// Predict using the neural network strategy\nauto stra = new NeuralNetwork();\nai.setStrategy(stra);\nai.decisionPredict();\n```\n\n## Template Method[ #](#template-method)\n\nThe Template Method pattern defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a superclass. Subclasses that inherit from it can override specific steps of the algorithm without changing its structure.\n\nFor example, in an editor application the basic operations can be designed into a superclass, while subclasses override methods to handle specific file types.\n\n![Template Method](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/11d0af43cb52.png)\n\nThe code:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass BasicEditor {\npublic:\n    void open() {\n        // Open file\n    }\n    void load() {\n        // Load file data\n    }\n    void close() {\n        // Close file\n    }\n    void save() {\n        // Save file\n    }\n    void find() {\n        // Search the file\n    }\n    void undo() {\n        // Undo\n    }\n    void redo() {\n        // Redo\n    }\n    void goto_() {\n        // Move the cursor\n    }\n    void select() {\n        // Select text\n    }\n};\n\nclass RtfEditor : public BasicEditor {\npublic:\n    void load() {\n        // Load rich-text data\n    }\n};\n\nclass DocEditor : public BasicEditor {\npublic:\n    void load() {\n        // Load a Doc document\n    }\n};\n\nclass PdfEditor : public BasicEditor {\npublic:\n    void load() {\n        // Load PDF document data\n    }\n    void save() {\n        // Saving is not supported; PDFs are view-only and cannot be modified\n    }\n    void undo() {\n        // Saving is not supported; PDFs are view-only and cannot be modified\n    }\n    void redo() {\n        // Saving is not supported; PDFs are view-only and cannot be modified\n    }\n};\n```\n\n## Visitor[ #](#visitor)\n\nThe Visitor pattern separates algorithms from the objects they operate on.\n\nWith this approach, the visited objects need no changes, yet their properties can still be accessed.\n\n![Visitor](/post/softwaredesignpattern4/a8db09ca9457.png)\n\nThe diagram shows an example that uses the Visitor pattern to fetch icon images from an application's menus and toolbars.\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nclass CtrlItem {\npublic:\n    Image* image() = 0;\n};\n\nclass ToolbarItem : public CtrlItem {\npublic:\n    Image* image() {\n        // Return the icon image\n    }\n};\n\nclass MenuItem : public CtrlItem {\npublic:\n    Image* image() {\n        // Return the icon image\n    }\n};\n\nclass Visitor {\npublic:\n    Image* visit(ToolbarItem*) = 0;\n    Image* visit(MenuItem*) = 0;\n};\n\nclass ImageVisitor : public Visitor {\npublic:\n    Image* visit(ToolbarItem* item) {\n        return item->image();\n    }\n    Image* visit(MenuItem* item) {\n        return item->image();\n    }\n};\n```\n\nUsing the Visitor pattern:\n\ncppCopy\n\n```cpp\nToolbarItem* item1 = new ToolbarItem(\"a.png\");\nToolbarItem* item2 = new ToolbarItem(\"b.png\");\nMenuItem* item3 = new MenuItem(\"c.png\");\n\nImageVisitor visitor;\nauto image1 = visitor.visit(item1);\nauto image2 = visitor.visit(item2);\nauto image3 = visitor.visit(item3);\n```\n","locale":"en","slug":"softwaredesignpattern4"}}